WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:07.700
For nine days, a team blamed a partner's gateway for emptying their documents. Emails, a call, an investigation.

00:00:07.700 --> 00:00:16.550
But the problem was on our side. Our relationship used an almost empty guideline, so Orderful dropped every field it did not recognise.

00:00:16.550 --> 00:00:21.200
One extra look would have saved a week. That look starts with the guideline.

00:00:21.200 --> 00:00:29.875
Think of an airline's baggage rules. Maximum size, maximum weight, what may go in the bag. The check-in desk measures every bag.

00:00:29.875 --> 00:00:39.600
A guideline is a partner's E D I rulebook, turned into a checklist. Orderful runs it on every document, and the result is Valid or Invalid.

00:00:39.600 --> 00:00:43.400
And an invalid outbound document is never delivered.

00:00:43.400 --> 00:00:49.950
So what does it check? For each segment: is it mandatory or optional, and how many times may it repeat?

00:00:49.950 --> 00:00:59.300
For each element: the data type, the length, and the allowed codes. A D T M date in version four oh ten must be exactly eight digits.

00:00:59.300 --> 00:01:07.775
And syntax notes, the rules between elements. On the N one segment, N one oh three and N one oh four must come as a pair.

00:01:07.775 --> 00:01:17.650
Now, who owns it? Every relationship has a leader, usually the big retailer. Its guideline is shared by every customer trading with that partner.

00:01:17.650 --> 00:01:25.450
So never edit, publish, or archive a partner's guideline. Fix our side, or ask the partner or Orderful support.

00:01:25.450 --> 00:01:33.675
When a document fails, read the error literally: the element, the value, and the rule it broke. Then check that row in the guideline.

00:01:33.675 --> 00:01:43.550
And open the transaction in both orgs. One team saw seventeen errors and blamed the supplier. In the supplier's org, that invoice was valid.

00:01:43.550 --> 00:01:48.250
Our own relationship was using the wrong guideline, copied by mistake.

00:01:48.250 --> 00:01:55.350
After any fix, retest on a new transaction. Validation results are frozen when a document arrives.

00:01:55.350 --> 00:02:04.700
If the guideline is ours and we change it, watch the publish trap. Publishing creates a new guideline with a new I D, and archives the old one.

00:02:04.700 --> 00:02:12.750
Your relationship stays on the old, archived version. So repoint it, and re-add the delivery channels, because they get wiped.

00:02:12.750 --> 00:02:18.750
One more lesson. Valid does not mean correct. A guideline only checks what it defines.

00:02:18.750 --> 00:02:26.175
A loose guideline let an empty skeleton ship notice pass as valid. The retailer would have rejected it on sight.

00:02:26.175 --> 00:02:35.650
Recap. A guideline is the partner's rulebook as a checklist. Diagnose in both orgs, fix our side, and repoint after every publish.

00:02:35.650 --> 00:02:39.675
Next, we'll write it all down, in mapping specs and crosswalks.

